// rts/docs/art/concept/round12/shots.json: shot list for concept art round 12 (from the developer's notes on round 11), read by rts/tools/ConceptArt/generate.py. Each shot has an id (the output file name), a size in pixels (width x height), a prompt, references (repository-relative images the result must stay faithful to, sent to the image edit endpoint) and, optionally, quality (low, medium or high; overrides the script's default for dense scenes). The style text is prepended to every prompt.
{
  // Shared style text prepended to every shot prompt. Source: CONCEPT_BRIEF.md, Style (low-poly rendering style decided in round 8).
  "style": "Science fiction strategy game art rendered as simple low-polygon 3D game models: chunky faceted geometry with visible flat polygons, plain hand-painted colours in broad areas, fully matte shading with soft ambient light only, no specular highlights, no reflections, no glow bloom, no fine surface detail. No logos, no borders, no watermark.",
  "shots": [
    {
      "id": "water_snail_poses",
      "size": "1536x1024",
      "references": ["rts/docs/art/concept/round11/water_snail_poses.jpg"],
      "prompt": "Redraw the two-pose design sheet in the reference image: the same low-polygon sea-snail artillery unit twice in clean side view on the same plain cool-grey backdrop, keeping the snail, its colours, the large cannon shell with its muzzle ring and panels, and the outside brace with its pivot disc exactly as they are. Three changes. First, in the firing pose on the right the cannon is not horizontal: it is rotated on its pivot to point diagonally upward at about 45 degrees, muzzle raised high toward the upper left, for a lobbed artillery shot. Second, it fires a bright turquoise ball of pressurised water and energy that arcs up and away in a curved lobbing trajectory, with a few flat water droplets; no dark ink anywhere. Third, the two eye stalks end in plain round eyes with no crosshair drawn on them; instead a small mechanical arm clamped to one eye stalk holds a round magnifying scope lens in front of that one eye, and the lens has a turquoise crosshair reticle on it; the other eye has no lens. The travelling pose on the left keeps the cannon upright and shows the same scope lens. No text. Only the snail, twice."
    },
    {
      "id": "ingame_engagement_choke",
      "size": "1536x1024",
      "quality": "high",
      "references": ["rts/docs/art/concept/round8/water_lineup_style_c_lowpoly.jpg", "rts/docs/art/concept/round9/skai_ground_lineup.jpg", "rts/docs/art/concept/round9/skai_air_units.jpg"],
      "prompt": "A realistic in-game screenshot of a modern competitive real-time strategy game in the middle of a big fight, the way such games actually look in play, using the low-polygon matte style and the unit designs of the reference images. Camera: the classic tilted three-quarter isometric RTS camera at normal play zoom, looking down at about 55 degrees; each soldier is small, about one twentieth of the screen height; not a flat plan view and not a close-up. Scene: two armies of thirty to forty units each collide at the bottom of a wide ramp between cliffs, beside shallow turquoise flood water with a clear shoreline. The fight is messy and in contact, never in rows: the front lines have crashed together in an irregular curved brawl, water-faction mantis shrimp fighters are tangled up in melee with Skai secretary-bird scouts, heron troopers are spread in a loose ragged arc behind them firing yellow energy bolts, an owl storm-caller calls lightning onto a clump of enemies, two Roc Riders and a Thunderhead bomber fly over the fight dropping charges, cuttlefish units shoot water jets up at the flyers, a tracked giant clam projects a turquoise dome shield over the centre of the water line, and two sea-snail cannons at the back lob turquoise water shells in high arcs that burst among the herons. Units face many different directions, some are flanking around the side, reinforcements trickle in from both edges, and wrecks and scorch marks from destroyed units lie on the ground. Skai are storm-charcoal with white feather tips and yellow lights; the water faction is muted sea-green with rose-coral bands. Game interface, drawn flat over the 3D scene like a real RTS: a small green or yellow or red health bar above every unit showing different amounts of damage; thin green selection rings on the ground under a group of about a dozen selected heron troopers; along the bottom edge a dark HUD bar with a square minimap of the whole map on the left showing coloured dots, a grid of small unit portrait icons in the centre, and a three-by-three grid of square command buttons with simple icons on the right; in the top right corner two small resource icons with numbers and a supply count. Part of a water-faction dome building is visible at the right edge. No words or sentences anywhere; only simple icons and digits."
    },
    {
      "id": "ingame_raid_expansion",
      "size": "1536x1024",
      "quality": "high",
      "references": ["rts/docs/art/concept/round8/water_lineup_style_c_lowpoly.jpg", "rts/docs/art/concept/round9/skai_ground_lineup.jpg", "rts/docs/art/concept/round9/skai_air_units.jpg"],
      "prompt": "A realistic in-game screenshot of a modern competitive real-time strategy game during a base raid, the way such games actually look in play, using the low-polygon matte style and the unit designs of the reference images. Camera: the classic tilted three-quarter isometric RTS camera at normal play zoom, looking down at about 55 degrees; each soldier is small, about one twentieth of the screen height. Scene: a water-faction expansion base on low ground partly covered by shallow turquoise flood water: a large spiral-shell drop-off building, three dome buildings, two small defence turrets, and a cluster of glowing resource crystals with a line of little octopus workers carrying cargo between the crystals and the drop-off. A Skai raiding party has just arrived: eight secretary-bird scouts with yellow foot blades are scattered among the workers cutting them down, three Roc Riders dive on the turrets from different directions, and the octopus workers are fleeing in all directions. The defenders are reacting, not waiting in formation: a handful of mantis shrimp fighters and two cuttlefish rush in from the right in a strung-out stream, a tracked giant clam is still rolling toward the fight with its dome shield half the base away, and one turret is already a burning wreck. Projectiles, water jets and impact flashes cross the scene; a few wrecked units lie on the ground. Skai are storm-charcoal with white feather tips and yellow lights; the water faction is muted sea-green with rose-coral bands. Cliffs and a ramp lead off the top of the screen, and the dark edge of unexplored fog of war covers the top-left corner. Game interface, drawn flat over the 3D scene like a real RTS: a small health bar above every unit and building showing different amounts of damage; thin green selection rings under the selected secretary-bird scouts; along the bottom edge a dark HUD bar with a square minimap on the left showing coloured dots and a red alert ping, a grid of small unit portrait icons in the centre, and a three-by-three grid of square command buttons with simple icons on the right; in the top right corner two small resource icons with numbers and a supply count. No words or sentences anywhere; only simple icons and digits."
    }
  ]
